kspkan 0.6.0 - a small mod manager for Kerbal Space Program

Read this first.  Everything here was written with an AI (vibecoded).
It was made for one person's computer and works there; it is not
tested anywhere else.  Read what a program does before you run it, and
use it with care.  Improvements are still welcome.

kspkan installs, removes and upgrades KSP mods from the CKAN module
list.  It is one static program in plain C89, with no libraries; the
only other program it runs is curl, to download.

    make && make install        builds, and installs to ~/.system/bin
    kspkan                      first start: finds the games on its own

The first start looks for the game where Steam, GOG and Heroic put it,
in every Steam library, and in your home directory a few levels down,
and remembers each copy it finds (it asks for the folder only when it
finds none).  Then  kspkan  opens a full screen mode on the game you
used last; Tab there lists the games with their mods and DLCs.  Press
? for the keys.  Everything works from the keyboard, and the mouse
works too.


COMMANDS

    kspkan update [FILE]            fetch the module list (FILE: a
                                    CKAN-meta .tar.gz fetched elsewhere)
    kspkan search [QUERY]           list modules (see SEARCH)
    kspkan show ID                  describe a module
    kspkan list                     installed modules, and those found
    kspkan scan                     find the mods already in the game
    kspkan compat [VER...|none]     other game versions whose mods fit
                                    too (CKAN's "compatible versions")
    kspkan install ID[=VER]...      install, with what they need (asks
                                    when several mods could fill one)
    kspkan remove ID...             remove, with what needs them
    kspkan upgrade [ID...]          upgrade installed modules
    kspkan autoremove               remove dependencies nothing needs
    kspkan keep ID... | auto ID...  mark as chosen by you / a dependency
    kspkan modlist [...]            saved lists of mods (see MODLISTS)
    kspkan clean                    delete downloaded archives
    kspkan add NAME [GAMEDIR]       remember a game folder
    kspkan instances                find games, list them with their
                                    mods and DLCs (* is the one in use)
    kspkan use NAME | forget NAME   choose one, or forget it
    kspkan version

OPTIONS

    -i NAME   instance                -g DIR   a game folder directly
    -k VER    game version            -a       ignore compatibility
    -t        allow testing releases  -f       overwrite unmanaged files
    -y        no questions            -R / -S  also recommended / suggested
    -p        most downloaded first   -m       no colours
    -r URL    another module list     -s DIR   all files in DIR (tests)

SEARCH

Words are ANDed; a leading - excludes a word.  Plain words match the
id, name, description and tags.

    author:x  tag:x  license:x  needs:x       the field contains x
    dl>5k  dl<100                            downloads
    after:2024-01-01  before:2025-01-01      release date
    size<5                                   under 5 MB unpacked
    is:installed  is:update  is:new
    is:chosen  is:dep  is:needed  is:orphan  (chosen by you; installed
                                  as a dependency; needed by another
                                  installed mod; a dependency nothing
                                  needs any more)
    sort:pop  sort:new  sort:small  sort:big  sort:name

Example:  kspkan search tag:plugin dl>20k size<2 sort:new


MODLISTS

A modlist is the set of mods you chose, saved as plain text, one
"id version" a line (# starts a comment).  Dependencies are not in it;
they are worked out again when it is installed.  Use one to set up a
new game folder like an old one, to keep a few sets of mods to switch
between, or to hand your set to somebody.

    kspkan modlist                  the saved ones
    kspkan modlist save NAME        save the mods you chose
    kspkan modlist show NAME        what it has, and how the game differs
    kspkan modlist install NAME     install what is missing (newest fit)
    kspkan modlist pin NAME         the same, at the versions it names
    kspkan modlist sync NAME        install it; remove chosen mods not in it
    kspkan modlist delete NAME
    kspkan modlist import FILE [NAME]  a list CKAN wrote (see below)

Where NAME is not a saved modlist, show, install, pin and sync take it
as a file: kspkan modlist install spelf.ckan  works on a file in the
current directory, as does any path.  A file CKAN wrote is first kept
as a modlist named after it (spelf); a plain "id version" list is just
used.

import reads a list that CKAN wrote and keeps it as a kspkan modlist
(NAME, or the file name without its extension).  kspkan only reads
CKAN's formats; it never writes them.  It understands:

    a .ckan modpack (File > Export modpack): its depends and
        recommends (any_of takes the first choice; suggests is left out)
    CKAN/registry.json from a game folder CKAN looked after: the mods
        installed there, without the ones CKAN put in as dependencies
    a saved mod list: plain text, Markdown, BBCode, CSV or TSV
    the output of "ckan list"

A pack made with CKAN often counts mods for older game versions as
fitting (CKAN's "compatible game versions"); import says so, and
kspkan compat 1.12 1.11 ...  does the same here (1.12 stands for every
1.12.x).  A mod of the list that still cannot go in (it fits no allowed
version, or clashes with another) is left out and named, and the rest
is installed.

Then  kspkan modlist show NAME  says what the game lacks, and install or
pin puts it in (or give the file to install straight away).  In the full
screen mode, o (or O) asks for a modlist or any of these files, with
Tab completing the path, and then how to apply it: a adds what the game
lacks on top, r also marks for removal the mods you chose that the list
does not have.  Nothing happens until Enter.  w writes the mods you
chose to a modlist name or to a file (a path with a /).


MODS ALREADY IN THE GAME

kspkan finds the mods that are in GameData already, put there by CKAN
or by hand, and marks them "found".  It does so on its own whenever
GameData or CKAN/registry.json has changed since it last looked, and
kspkan scan  does it by hand and says what it saw:

    found   MechJeb2 2.15.3.1           CKAN's registry, or matched
    found   Kopernicus (version not known)
    note    GameData/KerbalismConfig: took Kerbalism-Config-Default;
            it could also be Kerbalism-Config-RO LessRealKerbalism
    dlc     MakingHistory-DLC
    other   GameData/Principia (not in the module list; left alone)

What CKAN installed comes from CKAN/registry.json: the version, whether
it was a dependency, and the files still there.  A mod put in by hand
is matched by the directories and files its CKAN metadata would have
made; its version comes from a file name (ModuleManager.4.2.3.dll) or
its .version file, else it is "?" and  kspkan upgrade  puts the newest
in.  Found mods can be upgraded and removed like the others; their
files are only recorded, never moved.  The DLCs and directories the
module list does not know (Principia) are shown by list and scan and
never touched.  search is:found lists the found ones.


FULL SCREEN MODE

Each installed mod is marked i (chosen by you), d (installed as a
dependency) or f (found in the game), and u when a newer version fits.
The keys that matter most:

    i  I        show: all, installed, chosen by you, dependencies,
                unneeded dependencies, not installed, updates, marked
    p           the description at the bottom, on the right (90 columns
                or more), or off; remembered.  J K scroll it.
    Space       mark (+ install, x remove), Enter carries the marks out
    e           make an installed mod your choice, or a dependency
    o  O        import: a modlist, a kspkan modlist file, a .ckan
                modpack, CKAN/registry.json (or the game folder that
                has it), a list CKAN saved; Tab completes; then a: add
                on top, r: replace what you chose
    w           export the mods you chose: a modlist name or a path
    Tab         the games on this computer, found on their own, with
                their version, mods and DLCs; Enter uses one
    /  s  c     search, order, show mods that do not fit the game

Marks are kept on disk for each game: quitting, switching games,
fetching a new list or an install that fails never loses them.  When
some marked mods cannot go in (a clash, a missing dependency), the rest
are installed, the reasons are shown, and those stay marked.

The mouse: the wheel scrolls the list, or the right pane under the
pointer; a click picks a row; a double click, a right click or a click
in the mark column marks it; a click on a column heading changes the
order; the keys in the bottom bar can be clicked.  Hold Shift to select
text with the mouse, as in most terminals.

The right pane shows everything about the mod: the long description,
what it needs, recommends, suggests and conflicts with, which of your
mods need it, and its links.


WHAT IT DOES WITH MODS

  - depends, any_of, provides and conflicts, with version limits
  - recommends and suggests: offered when you install, never forced
  - install rules: find, find_regexp, file, as, filter, filter_regexp,
    include_only, find_matches_files; install_to GameData, Ships,
    GameRoot and the rest
  - every download checked by SHA-256 or SHA-1; mirrors tried in turn
  - removal deletes only the files kspkan recorded, then the
    dependencies nothing else needs
  - files kspkan did not install are never overwritten without -f
  - testing and development releases only with -t
  - the DLCs are detected from their folders

Not done: zip64 archives; offering providers of a virtual name in
recommends; importing archives you downloaded yourself; writing CKAN's
own formats (kspkan reads them, it never writes them).


FILES

$XDG_DATA_HOME/kspkan, or ~/.local/share/kspkan:

    instances            the remembered game folders
    index summary provides counts meta/    the module list (shared)
    cache/               downloaded archives
    modlists/            the saved modlists
    settings             where the full screen mode puts the description
    inst/NAME/installed  what was installed into that game
    inst/NAME/marks      the marks of the full screen mode

All are plain text.  Nothing is written into the game folder except the
mods themselves.


PRIVACY

kspkan contacts only the module list address and the download addresses
of the mods you install, through curl, which honours ALL_PROXY (so it
can go over Tor).  It sends nothing about you or your game, keeps no
log, and has no update check.


BUILDING

    make            static binary            make test     the tests
    make release    stripped static binary   make dist     source tarball
    make install    PREFIX=... MANDIR=...    make uninstall

Needs a C compiler, make, and libc.  The tests also need sh, python3
(only to build hostile test archives and serve them locally) and a
network connection, and use only scratch folders.  tests/test.sh needs
the module list first: run  kspkan update  once (it stops and says so
when there is none).


OTHER REPOSITORIES

kspkan is used on its own, or installed with the dotfiles
(./install.sh ksp there, which also installs Principia):

    https://github.com/AliceInUndernix/dotfiles     the Linux desktop
    https://github.com/AliceInUndernix/moonlander   the keyboard layout
    https://github.com/AliceInUndernix/dotterel     the steno theory


